Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 20:44:32 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] =?UTF-8?q?fix(ceos):=20give=20cEOS=20an=20empty=20block-d?=
 =?UTF-8?q?evice=20view=20=E2=80=94=20host=20disks=20decided=20whether=20a?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20lab=20could=20boot?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Sibling to 0013. Together they are one contract: a NOS may not CORRUPT host state
it does not own (0013, the sysctl leak), and it may not DEPEND on host state it
does not own (this).

The cEOS container runs privileged, so it inherits the NODE's block devices:
/sys/block and /dev are the host's. EOS boot walks that view unconditionally —
ssdinit.service -> ssdinit.sh -> mount_all_block_dev() enumerates every
/sys/block entry (whole disks AND partitions) and runs
`/sbin/initblockdev <dev> add --mountssd` for each; every one of those calls
shells out to a BARE `/sbin/blkid` (no device argument — a full probe of every
block device on the node) purely to read one LABEL.

So a single device that does not answer stalls the entire serial walk.
EOS.target never starts, Sysdb and ConfigAgent never come up, /usr/bin/Cli never
appears, and kubelet kills the container at the startup-probe budget
(48 x 5s = 240s) — forever, because every restart replays the same walk. The
container stays alive and exec-able throughout, which is why this reads as "the
NOS is not answering" rather than as a boot failure.

MEASURED 2026-08-15. The three bare-metal Supermicro workers each expose an
"ATEN Virtual CDROM" — the BMC/iKVM virtual-media device — as a USB sr0. SCSI
TEST UNIT READY to it never completes:

  blkid, D state:
    blk_execute_rq / scsi_execute_cmd / scsi_test_unit_ready
    / sr_drive_status / sr_block_ioctl   fd 6 -> /dev/sr0

  full `blkid`                sm-node-b 332s (>400s on another run) | worker-a 0.046s
  `blkid /dev/sr0`            sm-node-b 140s
  4KiB read under timeout 15  sm-node-b returned at 3m13s (D state cannot be
                              signalled) | worker-a "No medium found" in 0.018s

With 6 non-loop /sys/block entries that is ~33 MINUTES of serial scanning against
a 240s budget: cEOS could never boot on 3 of 5 nodes. A cEOS pod on sm-node-b was
still inside the walk 326s in, having advanced only from sda4 to sda3.

worker-a is NOT special because it has fewer devices — it has MORE (36
whole disks vs 1). It works because its sr0 answers instantly. The failure is not
device COUNT, it is whether any one device answers: an unownable property of the
host that lab placement must not depend on.

Second occurrence of this class. Backing /mnt/flash with a longhorn PVC created
one host /dev/sd* per device and stalled Sysdb the same way (task #77); that was
worked around by making /mnt/flash an emptyDir — by REDUCING the device count,
not by removing the dependency. The dependency is what is removed here.

FIX: mount an empty emptyDir over /sys/block. mount_all_block_dev() then
enumerates zero devices, initblockdev is never invoked, blkid is never run, and
EOS.target starts immediately — identically on every node. ssdinit.service still
RUNS and still SUCCEEDS (it simply finds nothing), so archive-init.service's
Requires= on it stays satisfied — which is why emptying its input was chosen over
masking the unit (systemd.mask=ssdinit.service was tested and works, but removes
only one consumer and breaks that dependency edge). Nothing in
cEOS-on-Kubernetes consumes the result: /mnt/flash is a Kubernetes volume,
ssdinit's SSD_DRIVE (/mnt/drive) does not exist in the image, and a booted cEOS
on worker-a has nothing mounted from the scan. /sys/class/net — the interface view
meshnet and the firewall permit hooks depend on — is a sibling of /sys/block and
is untouched, and so is /etc/sysctl.d, which carries 0013's guard.

VERIFIED with a probe-free cEOS pod (so kubelet could not mask the stall by
killing it) on a bare-metal worker AND on worker-a, same image, same args:

  sm-node-b, unmasked (negative control)  blkid wedged in D state; still in the
                                          walk at 326s; wfw -t 5 ConfigAgent
                                          Sysdb -> exit 1 "timed out"
  sm-node-b, masked                       Sysdb + ConfigAgent at 63s;
                                          wfw -> exit 0; Cli -p 15 answers
  worker-a,     masked (control)             Sysdb + ConfigAgent at 59s;
                                          wfw -> exit 0

Per-device kill switch, no CRD schema change, matching the
SKYFORGE_MGMT_RUNTIME_CONFIG precedent: spec.envvars
SKYFORGE_EOS_HOST_BLOCK_MASK=off. It does not disable 0013's guard, which stays
unconditional.

TestGetPodMasksHostBlockDevices and TestGetPodComposesBlockMaskWithSysctlGuard
both FAIL against a tree carrying 0013 but not this patch, and pass after;
0013's own TestSysctlGuardSuppressesHostGlobalLeak passes in both directions.
---
 controllers/ceoslabdevice_controller.go       |  94 ++++++++++-
 .../ceoslabdevice_controller_unit_test.go     | 155 ++++++++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 245 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)

diff --git a/controllers/ceoslabdevice_controller.go b/controllers/ceoslabdevice_controller.go
index 5180902..d16bbab 100644
--- a/controllers/ceoslabdevice_controller.go
+++ b/controllers/ceoslabdevice_controller.go
@@ -76,6 +76,68 @@ const (
 	sysctlGuardFile       = "99-zzskyforge-no-global-sysctl-leak.conf"
 	sysctlGuardMountPath  = "/etc/sysctl.d/" + sysctlGuardFile
 
+	// The cEOS container runs privileged, so it inherits the HOST's block-device
+	// view: /sys/block and /dev are the node's, not the pod's. EOS boot walks that
+	// view unconditionally — ssdinit.service -> ssdinit.sh -> mount_all_block_dev()
+	// enumerates every /sys/block entry (whole disks AND partitions) and runs
+	// /sbin/initblockdev <dev> add --mountssd for each, and EVERY one of those calls
+	// shells out to a bare `/sbin/blkid` (no device argument: a full probe of every
+	// block device on the node) just to read one LABEL. A single device that does not
+	// answer therefore stalls the whole serial walk, EOS.target never starts, Sysdb
+	// and ConfigAgent never come up, /usr/bin/Cli never appears, and kubelet kills the
+	// container at the startup-probe budget (48 x 5s = 240s) — forever, because every
+	// restart replays the same walk. The container stays alive and exec-able the whole
+	// time, which is why this reads as "the NOS is not answering" rather than as a
+	// boot failure.
+	//
+	// This is a SIBLING defect to the sysctl guard above, and the pair describes the
+	// whole contract: a NOS may not CORRUPT host state it does not own (the sysctl
+	// leak), and it may not DEPEND on host state it does not own (this). It has now
+	// bitten twice:
+	//
+	//  1. Backing /mnt/flash with a longhorn PVC created one host /dev/sd* per device;
+	//     the serial scan stalled Sysdb past the budget -> exit137 crashloop (task #77).
+	//     That was worked around by making /mnt/flash an emptyDir (see getVolumes) —
+	//     i.e. by REDUCING the device count, not by removing the dependency.
+	//  2. The three bare-metal Supermicro workers (sm-node-b/c/d) each expose an
+	//     "ATEN Virtual CDROM" — the BMC/iKVM virtual-media device — as a USB sr0.
+	//     SCSI TEST UNIT READY to it never completes: `blkid` parks in D state in
+	//     scsi_test_unit_ready/sr_drive_status. Measured 2026-08-15 on sm-node-b: a
+	//     full `blkid` took 332s (and did not return in >400s on another run), and a
+	//     single 4KiB read of /dev/sr0 under `timeout 15` returned only after 3m13s
+	//     because a D-state task cannot be signalled. With 6 non-loop /sys/block
+	//     entries that is ~33 MINUTES of serial scanning against a 240s budget, so
+	//     cEOS could never boot on 3 of 5 nodes — the platform's most common NOS,
+	//     unusable on the majority of the fleet.
+	//
+	// worker-a is NOT special because it has fewer devices — it has MORE (36
+	// whole disks vs 1). It works because its sr0 is a hypervisor "Linux Virtual CD/DVD"
+	// that answers instantly: a full `blkid` there completes in 0.046s and a read
+	// returns "No medium found" in 0.018s. So the failure is not device COUNT, it is
+	// whether any one device answers — an unownable property of the host that lab
+	// placement must not depend on.
+	//
+	// The fix is to stop the pod having a host block-device view at all: mount an empty
+	// emptyDir over /sys/block. mount_all_block_dev() then enumerates zero devices,
+	// initblockdev is never invoked, blkid is never run, and EOS.target starts
+	// immediately — identically on every node. ssdinit.service still RUNS and still
+	// SUCCEEDS (it simply finds nothing), so archive-init.service's Requires= on it
+	// stays satisfied; this is why masking the unit was rejected in favour of emptying
+	// its input. Nothing in cEOS-on-Kubernetes uses the result: /mnt/flash is a
+	// Kubernetes volume, ssdinit's SSD_DRIVE (/mnt/drive) does not exist in the image,
+	// and a booted cEOS on worker-a has nothing mounted from the scan. Verified on both a
+	// bare-metal sm-node and on worker-a: Sysdb + ConfigAgent warm at ~60s,
+	// `wfw -t 5 ConfigAgent Sysdb` exit 0, `Cli -p 15 'show version'` answers.
+	//
+	// /sys/class/net (the interface view meshnet and the firewall hooks depend on) is a
+	// SIBLING of /sys/block and is untouched by this mount. So is /etc/sysctl.d, which
+	// carries the sysctl guard above — the two mounts do not overlap.
+	//
+	// Per-device escape hatch, matching the SKYFORGE_MGMT_RUNTIME_CONFIG precedent and
+	// requiring no CRD schema change: spec.envvars SKYFORGE_EOS_HOST_BLOCK_MASK=off.
+	hostBlockMaskVolumeName = "eos-host-block-mask"
+	hostBlockMaskMountPath  = "/sys/block"
+
 	// Bringup pacing (ADAPTIVE): a large topology otherwise creates every CEosLab pod
 	// at once; the burst of per-pod meshnet CNI-ADD + veth wiring overwhelms containerd's
 	// sandbox creation (FailedCreatePodSandBox / DeadlineExceeded -> the CRI wedges and
@@ -1149,8 +1211,8 @@ func getPod(pod *corev1.Pod, device *ceoslabv1alpha1.CEosLabDevice, secretsAndCo
 	if err != nil {
 		return err
 	}
-	volumes := getVolumes(secretsAndConfigMaps, useStartupPersistence, device.Name)
-	volumeMounts := getVolumeMounts(secretsAndConfigMaps, useStartupPersistence, device.Name)
+	volumes := getVolumes(secretsAndConfigMaps, useStartupPersistence, device.Name, envVarsMap)
+	volumeMounts := getVolumeMounts(secretsAndConfigMaps, useStartupPersistence, device.Name, envVarsMap)
 	resources := getResourcesAPI(resourceMap)
 	startupProbe := getStartupProbeAPI(device)
 	readinessProbe := getReadinessProbeAPI(device, envVarsMap, useStartupPersistence)
@@ -1463,7 +1525,17 @@ func startupConfigName(deviceName string) string {
 	return fmt.Sprintf("%s-config", deviceName)
 }
 
-func getVolumes(secretsAndConfigMaps map[string]client.Object, useStartupPersistence bool, deviceName string) []corev1.Volume {
+// hostBlockMaskEnabled reports whether the cEOS container should be given an
+// empty block-device view instead of the node's. Default ON: a lab must boot the
+// same way on every node, and the alternative is a 240s crashloop whose only
+// symptom is "the NOS is not answering". Per-device kill switch via spec.envvars
+// SKYFORGE_EOS_HOST_BLOCK_MASK=off (no CRD schema change). See the
+// hostBlockMaskVolumeName comment for the measurements behind this.
+func hostBlockMaskEnabled(envVarsMap map[string]string) bool {
+	return !strings.EqualFold(strings.TrimSpace(envVarsMap["SKYFORGE_EOS_HOST_BLOCK_MASK"]), "off")
+}
+
+func getVolumes(secretsAndConfigMaps map[string]client.Object, useStartupPersistence bool, deviceName string, envVarsMap map[string]string) []corev1.Volume {
 	volumeNames := []string{}
 	for name := range secretsAndConfigMaps {
 		volumeNames = append(volumeNames, name)
@@ -1534,10 +1606,18 @@ func getVolumes(secretsAndConfigMaps map[string]client.Object, useStartupPersist
 			EmptyDir: &corev1.EmptyDirVolumeSource{},
 		},
 	})
+	if hostBlockMaskEnabled(envVarsMap) {
+		volumes = append(volumes, corev1.Volume{
+			Name: hostBlockMaskVolumeName,
+			VolumeSource: corev1.VolumeSource{
+				EmptyDir: &corev1.EmptyDirVolumeSource{},
+			},
+		})
+	}
 	return volumes
 }
 
-func getVolumeMounts(secretsAndConfigMaps map[string]client.Object, useStartupPersistence bool, deviceName string) []corev1.VolumeMount {
+func getVolumeMounts(secretsAndConfigMaps map[string]client.Object, useStartupPersistence bool, deviceName string, envVarsMap map[string]string) []corev1.VolumeMount {
 	volumeNames := []string{}
 	for name := range secretsAndConfigMaps {
 		volumeNames = append(volumeNames, name)
@@ -1592,6 +1672,12 @@ func getVolumeMounts(secretsAndConfigMaps map[string]client.Object, useStartupPe
 		SubPath:   sysctlGuardFile,
 		ReadOnly:  true,
 	})
+	if hostBlockMaskEnabled(envVarsMap) {
+		mounts = append(mounts, corev1.VolumeMount{
+			Name:      hostBlockMaskVolumeName,
+			MountPath: hostBlockMaskMountPath,
+		})
+	}
 	return mounts
 }
 
diff --git a/controllers/ceoslabdevice_controller_unit_test.go b/controllers/ceoslabdevice_controller_unit_test.go
index 39b8397..edce06f 100644
--- a/controllers/ceoslabdevice_controller_unit_test.go
+++ b/controllers/ceoslabdevice_controller_unit_test.go
@@ -991,3 +991,158 @@ func TestSysctlGuardSuppressesHostGlobalLeak(t *testing.T) {
 		}
 	}
 }
+
+// The cEOS container runs privileged and therefore inherits the NODE's block
+// devices. EOS boot walks them all (ssdinit -> initblockdev -> a bare `blkid`
+// per device), so one device that does not answer stalls Sysdb past the 240s
+// startup-probe budget and crashloops the pod forever — which is how cEOS became
+// unbootable on the three bare-metal workers whose BMC exposes a wedged "ATEN
+// Virtual CDROM" as sr0. Masking /sys/block with an empty emptyDir gives every
+// pod the same (empty) block-device view on every node.
+//
+// This test fails against a tree that has the sysctl guard but NOT this change:
+// it names the eos-host-block-mask volume specifically rather than asserting
+// that "some emptyDir exists", so the sysctl guard cannot satisfy it.
+func TestGetPodMasksHostBlockDevices(t *testing.T) {
+	for _, useStartupPersistence := range []bool{true, false} {
+		device := &ceoslabv1alpha1.CEosLabDevice{
+			ObjectMeta: metav1.ObjectMeta{Name: "leaf1", Namespace: "demo"},
+		}
+		pod := &corev1.Pod{}
+		if err := getPod(pod, device, map[string]client.Object{}, useStartupPersistence); err != nil {
+			t.Fatalf("persistence=%v: getPod() failed: %v", useStartupPersistence, err)
+		}
+
+		foundVolume := false
+		for _, volume := range pod.Spec.Volumes {
+			if volume.Name != hostBlockMaskVolumeName {
+				continue
+			}
+			foundVolume = true
+			if volume.EmptyDir == nil {
+				t.Fatalf("persistence=%v: %s volume is not an emptyDir: %+v",
+					useStartupPersistence, hostBlockMaskVolumeName, volume.VolumeSource)
+			}
+		}
+		if !foundVolume {
+			t.Fatalf("persistence=%v: getPod() did not declare the %s volume",
+				useStartupPersistence, hostBlockMaskVolumeName)
+		}
+
+		foundMount := false
+		for _, mount := range pod.Spec.Containers[0].VolumeMounts {
+			if mount.Name == hostBlockMaskVolumeName {
+				foundMount = true
+				if mount.MountPath != hostBlockMaskMountPath {
+					t.Fatalf("persistence=%v: %s mounted at %q, want %q",
+						useStartupPersistence, hostBlockMaskVolumeName, mount.MountPath, hostBlockMaskMountPath)
+				}
+				if mount.SubPath != "" {
+					t.Fatalf("persistence=%v: %s must mask the whole directory, got subPath %q",
+						useStartupPersistence, hostBlockMaskVolumeName, mount.SubPath)
+				}
+			}
+			// /sys/class/net is a sibling of /sys/block and carries the interface view
+			// meshnet and the firewall permit hooks depend on. Masking it would break
+			// the data plane, so assert we never mount anything else under /sys.
+			if strings.HasPrefix(mount.MountPath, "/sys/") && mount.MountPath != hostBlockMaskMountPath {
+				t.Fatalf("persistence=%v: getPod() mounted an unexpected path under /sys: %q",
+					useStartupPersistence, mount.MountPath)
+			}
+		}
+		if !foundMount {
+			t.Fatalf("persistence=%v: getPod() did not mask %s", useStartupPersistence, hostBlockMaskMountPath)
+		}
+	}
+}
+
+// The block mask and the sysctl guard are SIBLING halves of one contract — a NOS
+// may not depend on host state it does not own, and may not corrupt it either —
+// and they are built in the same two functions. They must COMPOSE: a pod carries
+// both volumes and both mounts, the sysctl guard keeps its SubPath (it lands as
+// one file inside /etc/sysctl.d, it does not replace the directory), and the
+// block mask keeps its whole-directory form. This is the test that fails if a
+// future edit to getVolumes/getVolumeMounts drops either one.
+func TestGetPodComposesBlockMaskWithSysctlGuard(t *testing.T) {
+	device := &ceoslabv1alpha1.CEosLabDevice{
+		ObjectMeta: metav1.ObjectMeta{Name: "leaf1", Namespace: "demo"},
+	}
+	pod := &corev1.Pod{}
+	if err := getPod(pod, device, map[string]client.Object{}, true); err != nil {
+		t.Fatalf("getPod() failed: %v", err)
+	}
+
+	volumes := map[string]corev1.Volume{}
+	for _, volume := range pod.Spec.Volumes {
+		volumes[volume.Name] = volume
+	}
+	for _, name := range []string{hostBlockMaskVolumeName, sysctlGuardVolumeName} {
+		volume, ok := volumes[name]
+		if !ok {
+			t.Fatalf("getPod() volumes = %v, want both %s and %s",
+				volumes, hostBlockMaskVolumeName, sysctlGuardVolumeName)
+		}
+		if volume.EmptyDir == nil {
+			t.Fatalf("getPod() %s is not an emptyDir: %+v", name, volume.VolumeSource)
+		}
+	}
+
+	mounts := map[string]corev1.VolumeMount{}
+	for _, mount := range pod.Spec.Containers[0].VolumeMounts {
+		mounts[mount.Name] = mount
+	}
+	blockMask, ok := mounts[hostBlockMaskVolumeName]
+	if !ok {
+		t.Fatalf("getPod() mounts = %v, want %s", mounts, hostBlockMaskVolumeName)
+	}
+	guard, ok := mounts[sysctlGuardVolumeName]
+	if !ok {
+		t.Fatalf("getPod() mounts = %v, want %s", mounts, sysctlGuardVolumeName)
+	}
+	if blockMask.MountPath != hostBlockMaskMountPath || blockMask.SubPath != "" {
+		t.Fatalf("block mask must cover the whole directory: path=%q subPath=%q",
+			blockMask.MountPath, blockMask.SubPath)
+	}
+	if guard.MountPath != sysctlGuardMountPath || guard.SubPath != sysctlGuardFile {
+		t.Fatalf("sysctl guard must stay a single-file SubPath mount: path=%q subPath=%q",
+			guard.MountPath, guard.SubPath)
+	}
+	// They must not shadow each other.
+	if strings.HasPrefix(guard.MountPath, hostBlockMaskMountPath+"/") {
+		t.Fatalf("sysctl guard %q lands under the block mask %q", guard.MountPath, hostBlockMaskMountPath)
+	}
+}
+
+// The mask is a per-device kill switch, not a build-time constant: an operator
+// must be able to hand a single device back its host block-device view without
+// rebuilding the controller. Turning it off must NOT disturb the sysctl guard —
+// that one is unconditional by design.
+func TestGetPodHostBlockMaskKillSwitch(t *testing.T) {
+	device := &ceoslabv1alpha1.CEosLabDevice{
+		ObjectMeta: metav1.ObjectMeta{Name: "leaf1", Namespace: "demo"},
+		Spec: ceoslabv1alpha1.CEosLabDeviceSpec{
+			EnvVar: map[string]string{"SKYFORGE_EOS_HOST_BLOCK_MASK": "off"},
+		},
+	}
+	pod := &corev1.Pod{}
+	if err := getPod(pod, device, map[string]client.Object{}, true); err != nil {
+		t.Fatalf("getPod() failed: %v", err)
+	}
+	sawGuard := false
+	for _, volume := range pod.Spec.Volumes {
+		if volume.Name == hostBlockMaskVolumeName {
+			t.Fatalf("getPod() declared %s despite SKYFORGE_EOS_HOST_BLOCK_MASK=off", hostBlockMaskVolumeName)
+		}
+		if volume.Name == sysctlGuardVolumeName {
+			sawGuard = true
+		}
+	}
+	if !sawGuard {
+		t.Fatalf("the block-mask kill switch must not disable the sysctl guard")
+	}
+	for _, mount := range pod.Spec.Containers[0].VolumeMounts {
+		if mount.MountPath == hostBlockMaskMountPath {
+			t.Fatalf("getPod() masked %s despite SKYFORGE_EOS_HOST_BLOCK_MASK=off", hostBlockMaskMountPath)
+		}
+	}
+}
